What I found by running the binary
• `yoyo --print "Reply with exactly: PONG" prints exactly PONG` and exits 0, as intended.
• `yoyo -p "..." still prints \nPONG\n\n. That is deliberate (plain -p passes the model's text through untouched, and a test pins it), but --help doesn't explain how -p differs from --print. People who capture -p` output in a script will still get the extra blank lines. This needs a decision: document it or trim it, not a quiet change.
• **Misleading trajectory rows:** a planned task that makes no commits (like the issue closures) shows up the same way as a task that stalled. `scripts/extract_trajectory.py` could tell the two apart.

